    cards: [
      ['Why do seasons change, and why does weather change?', "Seasons mainly result from Earth's tilted axis as it orbits the Sun, not changes in Earth-Sun distance. Day-to-day weather changes with moving air masses, pressure, temperature and moisture."],
      ['Why is Murree usually cooler than Lahore?', 'Murree is at a much higher altitude. In the lower atmosphere, temperature generally decreases with height as rising air expands and cools.'],
      ['Why can water pipes burst in winter?', 'Water expands when it freezes. If trapped in a pipe, the expanding ice can create enough pressure to split the pipe. Water is densest near +4°C, not -4°C.'],
      ['Why does wind blow?', 'Air moves in response to pressure differences produced partly by unequal heating. Rotation and friction also affect its direction and speed.'],
      ['What causes thunder?', 'Lightning heats the surrounding air rapidly. The sudden expansion creates a shock wave heard as thunder. Clouds do not need to collide.'],
      ['Why does a thrown object normally fall back down?', "Earth's gravity pulls it down. Ordinary throws do not give an object enough speed to escape Earth's gravity."],
      ['How do potential and kinetic energy differ?', 'Potential energy is associated with position or configuration. Kinetic energy is associated with motion.'],
      ['What is the difference between a dam and a barrage?', 'A dam commonly creates a reservoir to store water and may generate electricity. A barrage is a gated structure mainly used to regulate river level and divert flow, often into irrigation canals.'],
      ['What are isotopes?', 'Atoms of the same element with the same proton number but different neutron numbers, and therefore different mass numbers.'],
      ['What is specific heat capacity?', 'Energy needed to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 kelvin, equivalent to a 1°C temperature change. Unit: J/(kg·K).'],
      ['What does a voltmeter measure?', 'Potential difference between two points in a circuit, in volts.'],
      ['How do hard and soft water differ?', 'Hard water contains appreciable calcium or magnesium ions and forms lather with soap less readily. Soft water has low concentrations of these ions and lathers more easily.'],
      ['How does a rocket take off?', "It accelerates exhaust gas backward and receives an opposite thrust, consistent with Newton's third law and momentum conservation. It does not need to push against air."],
      ['What is pressure?', 'Normal force per unit area: P = F/A. The SI unit is the pascal (N/m²).'],
    ],
